n of 2.65%, I'll maintain consistency and use that value.\n\nI'm now comparing this to the 1943 data, specifically focusing on the February corporate bond yield of 2.68%. My benchmark remains at 2.60%, and I'm calculating the absolute deviations to understand the yield variations.\n\nThe deviations are:\n- 0.08 for Feb 1943\n- 0.05 for Feb 1944\n- 0.02 for Jan 1945\n\nAdding these gives a total of 0.15, which when divided by 3 yields an average deviation of 0.05. This precise calculation confirms my initial result.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_3b50b638bbae4e87ba6fa833","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"todo__todo_write","arguments":{"content":"- [x] Find High-Grade Corporate Bond yields for February 1943, February 1944, January 1945\n- [x] Extract yields from Treasury Bulletin documents\n- [x] Calculate absolute differences from benchmark (2.60%)\n- [x] Calculate average deviation (mean of absolute differences)\n- [x] Round to two decimal places and write to answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"todo"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
